Adding the YA is, as far as I reason, incorrect.  Google searches of
both r.g.r.n. (for YAAP's) and NAO (ascension dumplogs) didn't reveal
any other ascensions matching exactly what I set out to do.  I found
one similar YAAP (by nyra).  The devil is in the details, however
[1].  While looking all this up, I was shocked to learn that only 8 of
the 42 samurai ascension NAO dumplogs google found matched "katana"

For some time, I've wanted to do a stylish samurai.  My goal was to
ascend a samurai that only used my starting weapons (and was expert in
everything!).  Wielding ammunition to enchant/disenchant was ok.  This
was my fourth attempt at doing this.  The first try made it all the
way to Medusa's lair (no MR or refl) before being petrified by a
typographical error leading to a wardrobe malfunction (I took off my
blindfold ... I meant to apply my stethoscope).  The second try was
ended by a gnome lord with a wand of fire on Dlvl.07 (I knew I was
dead the moment he zapped).  The third was hell-hounded just outside
Delphi (Dlvl.09), but I made a mistake on that one.

After ascending all roles on NAO, I planned out a bunch of unusual/
uncommon weapon combinations that I wanted to do.  Between my primary
and secondary accounts on NAO, I've pulled off a few already.  This
all came about when I concluded that (with a few exceptions) doing a
God-B-Q for artifact weapons is a fairly pointless exercise.

While figuring all of this sillyness out, I realized something
interesting:  No other class has a general-purpose melee option that
does more normal damage than a katana+wakizashi samurai, unless
they're two-weaponing with an artifact.

Let me repeat that one more time:  No other class has a general-
purpose melee option that does more normal damage than a katana
+wakizashi samurai, unless they're two-weaponing with an artifact.

Only knights and valkyries can equal this combination, and then, only
if they're dual-wielding a pair of katanas.  Randomly finding one is
rare.  Finding two almost never happens.  A pair of elven broadswords
comes close, but is marginally inferior. [3]

